is a reviewer's call.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":333},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-9DMS","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh:1:type","kind":"test"}],"severity":"medium","title":"My reading of compat cf-8507c81c0f32c95a (SW-REQ-260922-9DMS): the same rename, same cause","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"I reproduced the engine's witness-name scan on both versions of the test file. A bash function `name() {` matches none of its patterns. At base nothing matches, so the witness falls back to `<file>:<line>`. At the head, the first match is the generic \"typed declaration\" pattern (`<type> <name>(`), which reads `if type(` at line 333 as a declaration named `type`. The keyword filter checks only the captured name, not `if`. Both compat findings follow from this. The fix belongs in the engine (skip heredoc bodies in shell files, or use the shell function pattern), not in this PR. This is my reading of the engine, not a package fact.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":333},{"id":"review.effects.approximations","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"Opinion (engine bounds): why a Lua line names a bash test's witness","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"review.behaviorDiff ran 213 inputs over 5 partitions with 0 differences. But its harness paths are shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml and MenuModel.js, and the PR touches neither. So base and head print the same differential output by construction. The configured differential has no partition for bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings: binds text in, picked row and dispatched action out. The facts that be