ndings","kind":"file","line":758},{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":598}],"severity":"info","title":"Removed behaviour 1: `awk -v selection=` read a backslash in a label as an escape","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"At base, two rows with the same text dispatched the first (`{ print; exit }`). At the head (line 762) a single match is dispatched. Two or more matches are dispatched (first row) only when none of them is `__lua`; otherwise nothing is printed and the script exits 1 with no message. The PR states this: \"When two rows read the same and one of them is a function bind, the pick is refused\". Tests at lines 625 and 637 pin it. Our stubbed run of the shipped config found no two rows that read the same.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings","kind":"file","line":762},{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":625},{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":637}],"severity":"info","title":"Removed behaviour 2: identical rows dispatched the first; now refused when one is a function bind","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings:dispatch_lua_function_binding","bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings:call_lua_function_if_marked"],"scenario":"live Hyprland 0.56.2 session: open Super+K, pick Reset zoom (a plain Lua function bind) after zooming to 1.5; expect zoom_factor 1 and no mark left in the Lua registry; repeat with a hyprctl reload between two picks"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Every test stubs `hyprctl`: the stub prints `arg: <ref>` for Lua binds and runs the dispatched Lua in a standalone `lua`. Nothing in the package executes against a compositor. Hyprland 0.56.2 prints the luaL_ref of a Lua bind as its arg (HyprCtl.cpp), and the PR author reports a live run: `Reset zoom` changed zoom_factor from 1.5 to 1. Our own headless attempt on Linux aborted at backend creation, so we have no live result. The live behaviour of `hl.dispatch(registry[ref])` and of the registry mark across a real reload needs a live test.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings","kind":"file","line":672},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings","kind":"file","line":676},{"id":"SW-REQ-261005-4MKB","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: does a pick run the function under a live Hyprland 0.56.2?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Live Hyprland 0.56.2: pick Reset zoom and Zoom in from Super+K\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings:dynamic_bindings","bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings:dispatch_binding"],"scenario":"user bindings.lua rebinds SUPER + SHIFT + F keeping the description 'File manager' to launch flea; picking that row from Super+K runs the shipped omarchy-launch-nautilus expression instead of flea"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"dynamic_bindings keeps the source expression whenever LUA_BIND_DISPATCHER_MAP has one for the row's mask, description and key (line 318). The replay stops at the first config error, before the user's bindings.lua, so the shipped expression is the one cached. In our stubbed run, `o.rebind(\"SUPER + SHIFT + F\", \"File manager\", { launch = \"flea\" })` still runs omarchy-launch-nautilus on this head and on quattro. With #7564 also applied it runs `uwsm-app -- flea`. This keying predates the PR, which says so under \"Not included\". It decides whether this PR alone closes the user-visible bug for rebinds.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings","kind":"file","line":318},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-0W96","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261005-4MKB","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: should a user rebind that keeps the shipped chord and description run the user's action?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Rebind keeping the shipped chord and description runs the user's action\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings:dynamic_bindings","bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings:dispatch_lua_function_binding"],"scenario":"user bindings.lua contains error(...) followed by o.bind('SUPER + ALT + P', 'Personal bind', 'true'); hyprctl binds reports it as __lua with ref 300; picking 'Personal bind' from the menu calls ref 300"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"On quattro the Lua replay stops at qconsole.lua, so no bind declared in the user's bindings.lua has a source expression. Hyprland still reports each of them as `__lua` with a ref. With this PR each one gets a ref-dispatch row: in our stubbed run, a new user function bind and a new user command bind both run through their ref. The PR description does not claim this. No test in the PR declares a bind after a config error, so once the replay gets further (e.g. with #7564), nothing pins this path. Our drafted test (in suggestedTests) passes on this head and fails with quattro's script.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings","kind":"file","line":322},{"id":"SW-REQ-261005-4MKB","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: are binds the source replay never reaches dispatched through their ref, and should a test pin it?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"A bind declared after a config error is called through its ref\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"mark_lua_state writes `registry.omarchy_menu_keybindings_marks[\"<pid>-<random>-<random>\"] = true` in the compositor's Lua state. Every path the script reaches removes it: the guarded call clears it, and a failed lookup runs unmark_lua_state. The tests check this with left_no_mark. A script killed between mark and call would leave one unused key until the next reload. The key is never matched again, so the consequence is a few bytes. Change effects were not analyzed, so no new_mutable_state fact exists to type this.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings","kind":"file","line":630},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-keybindings","kind":"file","line":678},{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":370}],"severity":"low","title":"Opinion (reading): the per-pick mark is new state in Hyprland's Lua registry","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"From the audit run (mcdc_coverage passed): keybindings-menu-test.sh witnesses 4MKB rows 1, 3, 4 and 5 (meets_policy true). Our proof work added the `# MCDC` markers (lines 393, 418, 456, 578) and the 3 new scenarios: undecodable identity, refused mark, and menu closed without a pick. The two remaining rows are `mcdc:ignore:defensive` (test lines 5-6, REVIEW-261005-THHB): a called function with the mark gone, and no call with one match and the mark present. Each needs the guard or the hand-off removed. These are requirement-level witnesses, not changed-line coverage.","refs":[{"id":"SW-REQ-261005-4MKB","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"mcdc_coverage","kind":"check"},{"id":"test/shell.d/keybindings-menu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":5}],"severity":"info","title":"SW-REQ-261005-4MKB MC/DC: 4 of 6 rows witnessed b